{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3002","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3002","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3002\",\"parent_revision\":\"4659a9080cc832a17ace841b7f069709f607c2668b60bbd55d0bd56269b0dc49\",\"root_revision\":\"4659a9080cc832a17ace841b7f069709f607c2668b60bbd55d0bd56269b0dc49\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-19\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThis repo's multi-agent orchestration model regularly runs many concurrent worktrees and interactive sessions against the same backlog/repo state (observed directly: `git worktree list` showed 20 worktrees and `ListAgents` showed 5 concurrent peer interactive sessions in a single snapshot during this session). Nothing structurally prevents two agents or sessions from picking up and working the same backlog item number simultaneously \u2014 coordination currently relies entirely on convention (worktree naming, one agent \\\"claiming\\\" an item by starting work on it) rather than an enforced, atomic mechanism.\\n\\nRead `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/sam_plan.py` around the `claim` command (~line 442, delegates to `operations.claim_task`) and `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py`'s `claim_task` (~lines 3195-3220, delegates to the backend's `claim` operation, e.g. `bd update --claim` for the beads backend) \u2014 verify these line numbers against current source, they may have shifted. `claim_task` is a read-modify-write operation with no compare-and-swap against the task's prior claim state: nothing rejects a second claim attempt on an already-claimed task from a different holder.\\n\\nThe atomicity primitive this needs already exists elsewhere in the same codebase and should be reused rather than reinvented: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/sync_state.py`'s `try_start()` (~line 118) implements a genuinely atomic slot-claim pattern already. Read it and evaluate whether `claim_task` can be rewritten to use the same underlying mechanism (or an equivalent compare-and-swap against the current claim holder) so that a claim attempt on an already-claimed task by a different holder is rejected rather than silently overwriting the prior claim.\\n\\n**Candidate direction** (not a required approach \u2014 investigate before committing): make `claim_task` check the task's current claim-holder field before writing; if already claimed by a different holder, return/raise an explicit \\\"already claimed by X\\\" outcome instead of silently succeeding; enforce this check at the SAM dispatch boundary (wherever a task is actually handed to an agent for execution), not just at the MCP tool surface, so a caller can't bypass it by going around `claim_task`.\\n\\nThis is a P2: a real structural gap with plausible impact (duplicate concurrent work, wasted agent-hours, potential conflicting writes), but no specific incident of two agents actually colliding on the same item has been confirmed yet in this repo's history \u2014 flag that as an open question for whoever grooms this item to check (e.g. via git/PR history for signs of duplicate-effort commits on the same issue number).\\n\\nRelated but distinct existing items found during duplicate-check: #1479 (SAM MCP `sam_claim` \u2014 agent identity tracking on `sam_schema/core/query.py::claim_task`, a different claim_task than this item's target); #912 (TaskBackend storage migration \u2014 documents ADR-2509-3, which explicitly *accepts* non-atomic claims at the TaskBackend/GistTaskLayer layer and defers exactly-once guarantees to caller-side serialization \u2014 a conflicting existing architectural decision worth reconciling against this item's proposal); #2549/#2573 (startup-sync `try_start()` usage in `sync_state.py` \u2014 same primitive, unrelated use case). None of these covers rewriting `backlog_core/operations.py::claim_task` itself to use a compare-and-swap pattern.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] `GitHubTaskProvider.claim_task` (`sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py`) uses a genuine compare-and-swap (e.g. conditional label update guarded by a re-read, or an equivalent optimistic-concurrency check) instead of an unconditional read-then-write\\n- [ ] A concurrency test for `GitHubTaskProvider.claim_task` exists, following the pattern in `test_sqlite_content_task_providers_claim_once_from_concurrent_snapshots` (`sam_schema/tests/test_content_task_provider_transactions.py:279`)\\n- [ ] Either the `sam plan claim` CLI / `sam_task claim` MCP entrypoint enforces single-writer semantics itself (so it is safe regardless of caller), or ADR-2509-3 is updated to explicitly document that direct CLI/MCP claim calls outside the `implement-feature` dispatch loop are an unsupported/unsafe usage pattern, with that constraint surfaced in the command's help text and MCP tool description\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **close the two concrete gaps in `claim_task` atomicity that ADR-2509-3 does not cover** so that **concurrent claim attempts on GitHub-backed SAM plans, and claims made outside the dispatch orchestrator, cannot silently double-claim a task**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session observation 2026-08-19 \u2014 git worktree list showed 20 concurrent worktrees, ListAgents showed 5 concurrent peer interactive sessions in one snapshot\\n- **Priority**: P2 (unchanged \u2014 real but narrower than originally scoped; no confirmed incident of an actual collision found in repo history)\\n- **Scope narrowed**: 2026-08-19, after verifying ADR-2509-3 covers the `local`/Gist path (see comment). Original broader \\\"claim_task lacks CAS\\\" framing is resolved for that path.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"a6a0168a6a26ebdffc605fbc630ba000641c9d8b29820e9e7e9259095dc59faf\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPiIbOw\"}"}